THE HUMAN KNOWLEDGE REGISTRY (HKR)

Enforceable Architecture for a Planetary Epistemic Registry

Theoretical Specification v0.4 — Revised, Validated, and Operational

---

Abstract

This specification revises the Human Knowledge Registry (HKR) from a descriptive philosophy of the registry into an enforceable epistemic architecture. The v0.3 thesis preserved distinctions on paper — assertion vs. evaluation, ingestion vs. visibility, claim vs. assessment — but provided no mechanism enforcing them under adversarial scale. Following comparison with Eigenius (commit-time epistemic stratification), ConVer-G/QuaQue (concurrent graph versioning with bitstring storage), AIDA (controlled claim grammar), and nanopublications (container structure at scale), we reconstruct the HKR around five enforceable kernel rules, a profiled claim grammar, status-as-Assessment, an Act interchange protocol for federation, and a stratified self-hosting model. We adopt the position that the HKR is an index of readings, not a knowledge base: canonical content is the source text, and every formalization is a recorded transformation Act. We specify the adversarial test battery as executable code, not prose. The Planetary State Core (PSC) is formalized strictly as a materialized projection  \mathrm{PSC}_t = \pi_{\mathrm{Earth},\tau=t,\sigma}(\mathrm{HKR}) , with the constraint that every PSC value must exhibit a complete provenance chain or be flagged as PSC_INCONSISTENCY.

Keywords: epistemic audit, commit-time invariants, nanopublications, AIDA, PROV-O, provenance, stratified knowledge graphs, federation, adversarial validation.

---

1. Introduction and Positioning

1.1 The Central Question

\boxed{
\text{Can a persistent epistemic registry preserve the distinction between what was}
}

\boxed{
\text{asserted, observed, evaluated, contested, surfaced, superseded, and documented}
}

\boxed{
\text{— at planetary scale, under adversarial conditions, without collapsing them?}
}

The v0.3 specification answered conceptually yes. This specification answers operationally how — or demonstrates where enforcement is impossible.

1.2 Position Against Validated Literature

The HKR does not compete with existing standards; it orchestrates them under enforceable rules. Specifically:

· Nanopublications solved the container: assertion + provenance + publication info, scaled beyond 10^7 instances, primarily in life sciences. The HKR adopts nanopublication structure for atomic publishable units but extends it with typed Acts and stratified status.
· PROV-O standardizes provenance (Entity, Activity, Agent). The HKR maps every Act to a PROV-O Activity subclass.
· AIDA provides a controlled natural language for claims (Atomic, Independent, Declarative, Absolute). The HKR adopts AIDA for level C1 representation.
· Eigenius enforces epistemic categories (declared | observed | derived | verified) as commit-time invariants with universe stratification. The HKR adopts this enforcement model directly.
· ConVer-G / QuaQue provides condensed relational versioning with bitstrings and SPARQL→SQL translation. The HKR adopts this for temporal storage.
· Seven-tier epistemic frameworks demonstrate that status is dynamic, multi-actor, genealogical — not a mutable field. The HKR replaces status-fields with status-Assessments.

1.3 What the HKR Does Not Claim

\mathrm{HKR} \neq \text{all human knowledge}, \quad \mathrm{HKR} \neq \text{truth}, \quad \mathrm{HKR} \neq \text{consensus}, \quad \mathrm{HKR} \neq \text{oracle}.

The HKR claims only to be  \text{persistent} \wedge \text{versioned} \wedge \text{provenance-aware} \wedge \text{evidence-linked} \wedge \text{conflict-preserving} .

