2. The Enforceable Kernel

2.1 Principle of Enforceability

Every invariant must be checkable at write time or detectable by query. An invariant that neither rejects a write nor produces a detectable query result is not an invariant — it is a comment.

Formally, for each invariant  I_j :

\forall\, \text{write } w, \quad \neg I_j(w) \;\Rightarrow\; \big(\text{Reject}(w) \;\vee\; \text{Flag}(w, \texttt{VIOLATION}_j)\big).

2.2 The Five Kernel Rules

The entire architecture reduces to five rules enforced at commit time:

K1 — Write is Act.
Every write to the registry is an Act  A  with:

A = (p, \tau, m, \text{inputs}, \text{outputs}, \sigma)

where  p  is producer,  \tau  timestamp,  m  method (versioned), inputs is the set of referenced objects, outputs the created objects,  \sigma  signature. No write occurs outside an Act.

K2 — No Update; Only Supersede.

\forall o \in \mathcal{O}, \;\; \text{Update}(o) = \bot, \quad \text{Supersede}(o, o', A) = \top

with  A  an Act and  o' \xrightarrow{\texttt{supersedes}} o  a typed relation. The prior object remains queryable at any  \tau < \tau(A) .

K3 — Assessment Requires Criterion.

\forall a \in \mathrm{Assessment}, \;\; a.\text{criterion} \neq \bot \;\wedge\; a.\text{criterion}.\text{version} \neq \bot.

An Assessment without a versioned criterion is rejected.

K4 — Query Returns Provenance.

\forall q, \;\; q \mapsto (r, A_{\mathrm{scope}}(q), \pi(q))

where  A_{\mathrm{scope}}  is the ScopingAct and  \pi  the visibility policy under which  q  executed.

K5 — Formalization is Act.

\forall C_i, C_j, \;\; C_i \xrightarrow{\texttt{formalizes}} C_j \;\Rightarrow\; \exists A, \;\; A.\text{type} = \texttt{Formalization} \wedge A.\text{outputs} \ni C_i.

No formalization exists without its transformation Act.

These five rules are implementable on PostgreSQL + RDF in bounded time. Anything not reducible to them is deferred.

2.3 Rejection of the 15-Register Model

The fifteen registers proposed in v0.3 are descriptive, not enforceable. This specification retains a minimal object typology:

\mathcal{O} = \{\mathrm{Artifact}, \mathrm{Entity}, \mathrm{Claim}, \mathrm{Observation}, \mathrm{Model}, \mathrm{Question}, \mathrm{Gap}, \mathrm{Assessment}, \mathrm{Representation}, \mathrm{Norm}, \mathrm{Act}\}.

but defers refinement until the kernel runs.

