3. Claim Grammar

3.1 The Canonicality Theorem

Theorem 1 (Canonicality). The canonical content of a Claim is its source text at level C0. Every formalization  C1, C2, C3  is a recorded reading and is queryable only relative to its transformation Act.

Formally:

\mathrm{Canonical}(C) = C_0, \qquad C_n \;\text{is a reading of}\; C \;\text{iff}\; \exists A_n:\; A_n.\text{type} = \texttt{Formalization}_n \wedge A_n.\text{input} = C_0.

Consequences:

1. Queries are formalization-relative:  q(\varphi_{C2}) \neq q(\varphi_{C3}) .
2. The HKR is an index of readings, not a knowledge base.
3. Every transformation  C_i \to C_j  is an Act with producer, method, version, timestamp.

3.2 The Four Representation Levels

C_0 \;\xrightarrow{A_1}\; C_1 \;\xrightarrow{A_2}\; C_2 \;\xrightarrow{A_3}\; C_3

with each  A_k  an Act. The graph is a DAG, not a chain: multiple  C_1  readings may coexist; a  C_2  may derive from multiple  C_0 ; the reverse  C_2 \to C_0  is a distinct Act of verification.

Level Content Method
 C_0  raw text source
 C_1  AIDA-structured claim controlled natural language
 C_2  formal semantics (scope, condition, exception, modality) domain ontology
 C_3  computable representation executable schema

3.3 Modality: Content vs. Act

\mu_{\text{content}}(C) \neq \mu_{\text{act}}(A).

Closed \mu_{\text{act}} vocabulary (six elements, mapped to PROV-O Activity subclasses):

\mu_{\text{act}} \in \{\texttt{assert}, \texttt{hypothesize}, \texttt{report}, \texttt{endorse}, \texttt{contest}, \texttt{retract}\}.

Content modality  \mu_{\text{content}}  is deferred to domain profiles; the HKR does not invent a universal vocabulary. For scientific claims, adopt the AIDA-derived attribute set: scope, condition, exception, temporal_scope, spatial_scope, evidence_basis.

3.4 Empirical Validation Requirement

Requirement R1. Before any domain ontology is frozen, conduct a double-annotated pilot: 500 claims extracted from 50 papers in a single domain (boreal fire ecology), annotating  C_0 \to C_1  on scope, condition, exception, modality. Report Cohen's  \kappa  per field.

Acceptance threshold:

\kappa_{\text{exception}} \geq 0.6, \quad \kappa_{\text{scope}} \geq 0.6, \quad \kappa_{\text{condition}} \geq 0.6.

If  \kappa < 0.6  on exceptions, the claim grammar fails empirically and Chapter 4 (below) is invalidated.

Literature precedent: claim-extraction inter-annotator studies report  \kappa  between 0.334 and 0.730 depending on task complexity. The threshold is calibrated to the upper half of that range, appropriate for structured claim fields.

