4. Epistemic Acts

4.1 The Act as First-Class Object

Every epistemic operation is an Act:

A = (p, \tau, m, \text{inputs}, \text{outputs}, \sigma, \texttt{prov\text{-}type})

with prov-type a PROV-O Activity subclass. Act subtypes:

\mathcal{A} = \{\texttt{Assert}, \texttt{Hypothesize}, \texttt{Report}, \texttt{Endorse}, \texttt{Contest}, \texttt{Retract}, \texttt{Assess}, \texttt{Scope}, \texttt{Surface}, \texttt{Formalize}, \texttt{Extract}, \texttt{Replicate}, \texttt{Translate}, \texttt{Merge}\}.

4.2 The ScopingAct

The ScopingAct closes the upstream provenance gap of all current RAG systems:

```
ScopingAct
  ├── query:              raw formulation
  ├── interpretation:     question effectively addressed
  ├── scope:              entities, dates, domains
  ├── window_justification: why this temporal window
  ├── retrieval_policy:   method, version, thresholds, filters
  ├── candidates:         objects examined
  ├── selected:           objects retained
  ├── excluded:           objects discarded + reason
  └── producer:           agent + version
```

Enforcement (per K4): every query returns its ScopingAct id.

4.3 The SurfaceAct

I(K) \neq V(K).

Ingestion is universal; visibility is an Act:

```
SurfaceAct
  ├── target_object
  ├── producer
  ├── criterion
  ├── rank_score:         numeric, logged
  ├── rank_method:        versioned
  ├── timestamp
  └── contestability
```

Enforcement (per K3 analog): SurfaceAct requires a versioned rank method. No binary-only inclusion.

