5. Status as Assessment

5.1 Refutation of Status-as-Field

v0.3 attached a status trajectory to each Claim:

\text{proposed} \to \text{reported} \to \text{supported} \to \text{replicated} \to \text{consolidated}

This conflates social process with evidential state and violates the thesis's own principle  \mathrm{Claim} \neq \mathrm{Assessment} . The seven-tier literature confirms status is dynamic, multi-actor, genealogical.

5.2 Status-as-Assessment

Rule S. No status field exists on any Claim. Status is a query over timestamped Assessments.

\mathrm{status}(C, t, \text{criterion}) = \mathrm{fold}\big(\{a \in \mathrm{Assessment} : a.\text{object} = C \wedge a.\tau \leq t \wedge a.\text{criterion} = \text{criterion}\}\big)

An Assessment has:

```
Assessment
  ├── object:        X
  ├── claim_type:    "support_level" | "replication_status" | "dispute_status" | ...
  ├── value:         enumerated
  ├── criterion:     versioned method
  ├── produced_by:   Act reference
  ├── produced_at:   timestamp
  └── inputs:        [Act ids]
```

Consequence: entire class of silent overwrites eliminated. Query supported(X, t) translates to a query over Assessments, never to a mutable attribute.

