7. Federation Protocol

7.1 Act Interchange Format

Each HKR instance  \mathcal{H}_i  exposes Acts in JSON-LD mapped to PROV-O:

```json
{
  "@context": "https://www.w3.org/ns/prov",
  "@type": "prov:Activity",
  "subtype": "Assert",
  "id": "content-hash",
  "producer": "did:web:instance.example",
  "timestamp": "ISO-8601",
  "inputs": ["content-hash", ...],
  "outputs": ["content-hash", ...],
  "method": {"name": "...", "version": "..."},
  "signature": "..."
}
```

Content-addressed IDs ensure identity across instances.

7.2 Refuse Act

Each instance may refuse Acts from other instances:

```
RefuseAct
  ├── target_act:      content-hash
  ├── reason:          enumerated
  ├── producer:        instance id
  └── timestamp
```

Refusals are themselves Acts and are queryable.

7.3 Divergence Query

\texttt{divergent}(e) = \{(o_1, i_1), (o_2, i_2), \ldots\}

where each  o_j  is a competing resolution of entity  e  from instance  i_j .

Divergence is represented as an object, not as a bug. Reconciliation is an Act (Merge), requires multi-instance endorsement, and is itself traceable.

7.4 Governance of  \pi 

Visibility policy  \pi  is a social object. The HKR:

· makes  \pi  explicit (stored as a Norm object);
· traces applications of  \pi  (via K4);
· allows replay under  \pi' :  \texttt{query}(q, \pi') ;
· records contestations of  \pi  as Acts;
· requires multi-producer endorsement for  \pi -changes.

Economic requirement: ingestion incentives (citation credit via nanopublication model), curation incentives (reviewed ScopingActs as citable outputs). Without incentives, the registry will be empty or captured.

