8. Epistemic Stratification (Self-Hosting)

8.1 The Paradox

The HKR asserts facts about its own objects (e.g., "C was asserted by A at  \tau "). If these assertions are in the HKR, the system is self-referential.

8.2 Stratified Solution (Eigenius Model)

Rule St. No Level  N  object asserts about Level  \geq N .

· Level 0: domain claims (about the world).
· Level 1: Acts about Level 0 (assertions, Assessments).
· Level 2: registry operations about Level 1 (SurfaceActs, ScopingActs).

Enforced at commit time. prov-type determines stratum; cross-stratum writes with upward reference are rejected.

Consequence: "the HKR's claims about itself" are queryable at Level 1, without paradox.

---

9. The PSC Projection

9.1 Formal Statement

\mathrm{PSC}_t = \pi_{\mathrm{Earth}, \tau=t, \sigma}(\mathrm{HKR})

where:

·  \pi_{\mathrm{Earth}}  restricts to Earth-system entities;
·  \tau = t  restricts validity to time  t ;
·  \sigma  is the projection specification (admissible types, aggregation rules, spatial/temporal resolution).

9.2 The Provenance Chain Requirement

Requirement R2 (PSC Completeness). Every value  v \in \mathrm{PSC}_t  must exhibit:

> v \;\leftarrow\; \text{projection\_spec} \;\leftarrow\; \text{aggregation\_method} \;\leftarrow\; \{\text{observations}, \text{datasets}, \text{models}, \text{assessments}\}
> 

If this chain is incomplete:

> \mathrm{flag}(v) = \texttt{PSC\_INCONSISTENCY}
> 

not "missing data".

9.3 No Authority Between Systems

\mathrm{PSC} \not\succ \mathrm{HKR}, \quad \mathrm{HKR} \not\succ \mathrm{PSC}.

They represent two epistemic spaces connected by projection and correspondence. Neither decides for the other.

