13. Conclusion

The v0.4 specification reframes the HKR from a philosophy of the registry to an enforceable epistemic architecture. Its central moves are:

1. Five kernel rules replace fifteen descriptive registers.
2. Canonicality Theorem fixes the HKR as an index of readings; every formalization is an Act.
3. Status-as-Assessment eliminates status-fields and the class of silent-overwrite failures.
4. ScopingAct closes the upstream provenance gap of all RAG systems.
5. Epistemic stratification resolves the self-hosting paradox at commit time.
6. Adversarial battery as code validates each invariant by attempted violation.
7. PSC as strict projection with the completeness requirement (R2).

The research question remains:

\boxed{
\text{Can distinctions survive scale?}
}

The answer v0.4 proposes: only if the architecture enforces them at commit time, and proves enforcement through executable tests. The five kernel rules, the twelve adversarial tests, and the five-step research program are the operational answer. Everything else is deferred until these hold.

