Appendix A: Mathematical Foundations

A.1 Notation and Conventions

We fix the following notation throughout:

Symbol Meaning
\mathcal{H} The HKR system as a whole
\mathcal{O} Set of object types
\mathcal{R} Set of typed relations
\mathcal{A} Set of epistemic acts
\mathcal{T} Temporal domain (totally ordered set)
\mathcal{S} Epistemic status lattice
\mathcal{Q} Query language
K_i A Knowledge Object
C A Claim
A An Act
\pi Visibility policy
\sigma Projection specification
\tau Timestamp / validity time

A.2 The Knowledge Object

Definition A.1 (Knowledge Object). A Knowledge Object is a tuple:

K_i = (I, C, E, P, M, R, T, S, Q)

where:

· I = persistent identity (content-addressed hash)
· C = content (text, structure, or both)
· E = evidence set (references to other objects)
· P = provenance (references to Acts)
· M = method (versioned reference)
· R = relations (typed edges to other objects)
· T = temporal validity interval [t_{\text{start}}, t_{\text{end}})
· S = epistemic status (derived, not stored — see §A.6)
· Q = quality/uncertainty metadata

A.3 The Act

Definition A.2 (Epistemic Act). An Act is:

A = (p, \tau, m, \text{inputs}, \text{outputs}, \sigma, \texttt{prov-type})

where:

· p \in \mathcal{A}\text{gents} = producer
· \tau \in \mathcal{T} = timestamp
· m = method (versioned)
· \text{inputs} \subseteq \mathcal{O} = referenced objects
· \text{outputs} \subseteq \mathcal{O} = created objects
· \sigma = cryptographic signature
· \texttt{prov-type} ∈ PROV-O Activity subclasses

The six illocutionary act types are:

\mu_{\text{act}} \in \{\texttt{assert}, \texttt{hypothesize}, \texttt{report}, \texttt{endorse}, \texttt{contest}, \texttt{retract}\}

A.4 Provenance Semirings

Following Green, Karvounarakis, and Tannen (2007), provenance for positive relational algebra is captured by commutative semirings. For SPARQL, Geerts et al. (2016) extended this to spm-semirings (SPARQL Minus semirings) to handle non-monotone operators:

Definition A.3 (spm-semiring). A structure \kappa = (K, +_\kappa, \times_\kappa, -_\kappa, 0_\kappa, 1_\kappa) where (K, +_\kappa, \times_\kappa, 0_\kappa, 1_\kappa) is a commutative semiring and -_\kappa is a unary operation satisfying:

-(x +_\kappa y) = -x -_\kappa -y

x \times_\kappa -x = 0_\kappa

Spm-semirings guarantee more compact explanations and native support for non-monotonic SPARQL operators. The provenance of a query result is an element of the spm-semiring, computed by structural recursion over the query plan.

A.5 Bitstring Versioning Algebra

Following Puget Gil et al. (2026), versioning information is encoded as bitstrings.

Definition A.4 (Versioned Quad). A versioned quad is:

q = (s, p, o, g, \text{bits})

where \text{bits} \in \{0,1\}^n and n is the number of snapshots. Bit i is 1 iff q exists in snapshot i.

Operations:

· Union: \text{bits}_1 \lor \text{bits}_2 (bitwise OR)
· Intersection: \text{bits}_1 \land \text{bits}_2 (bitwise AND)
· Difference: \text{bits}_1 \land \neg \text{bits}_2 (bitwise AND NOT)
· Selection at version i: \text{bit}_i

SPARQL queries are translated to SQL over the versioned_quad table using PostgreSQL's bit varying type and bitwise operators.

A.6 Status as Assessment (Not Field)

Definition A.5 (Assessment). An Assessment is:

a = (\text{object}, \text{claim\_type}, \text{value}, \text{criterion}, \text{produced\_by}, \text{produced\_at}, \text{inputs})

Definition A.6 (Status Function). For a claim C, time t, and criterion \gamma:

\text{status}(C, t, \gamma) = \text{fold}\big(\{a \in \text{Assessment} : a.\text{object} = C \wedge a.\tau \leq t \wedge a.\text{criterion} = \gamma\}\big)

No status field exists on any Claim. Status is a query over timestamped Assessments.

A.7 Epistemic Stratification

Following Eigenius (Will et al., 2026):

Definition A.7 (Stratum). For an object o:

\text{stratum}(o) = \min\{n : o \text{ asserts only about objects at level} < n\}

· Level 0: domain claims (about the world)
· Level 1: Acts about Level 0
· Level 2: registry operations about Level 1

Rule St. No Level N object asserts about Level \geq N. Enforced at commit time.

A.8 Temporal Logic for Knowledge Graphs

Following the temporal KG literature:

Definition A.8 (Temporal Knowledge Graph). A temporal KG is a tuple G = (T, \mathcal{F}, \rho) where T is a time domain, \mathcal{F} is a set of temporal facts, and \rho: \mathcal{F} \to 2^T assigns validity intervals.

Temporal operators (LTL-based):

· \diamondsuit_F \varphi = "sometime in the future \varphi"
· \diamondsuit_P \varphi = "sometime in the past \varphi"

Temporal conjunctive queries (TCQs) are built inductively from conjunctive queries via LTL operators.

A.9 Justification Logic

Following Artemov (2008), justification logic extends epistemic logic with justification terms:

t : F

read "t is a justification for F." Instead of \Box X, one writes t : X. Justifications are not assumed to be factive — no logical truths are assumed a priori as justified for the agent. This provides the formal foundation for the HKR's distinction between assertion and evidence: an assertion is a claim with a justification term, not a claim that is "true."

A.10 Content-Addressed Storage

Definition A.9 (Content-Addressed Identity). For an object o:

\text{ID}(o) = H(\text{canonical\_serialization}(o))

where H is a cryptographic hash (SHA-256). Properties:

· Immutability: changing o changes \text{ID}(o)
· Verifiability: anyone can recompute \text{ID}(o)
· Decentralization: identity is independent of location

