D.2 Citation Laundering Detection

Rule T5: If A \xrightarrow{\texttt{cites}} B \xrightarrow{\texttt{cites}} C and C is retracted, then both A and B must be flagged as potentially invalidated.

Detection algorithm:

1. Build citation graph from evidence relations
2. Mark retracted objects
3. Propagate invalidation via transitive closure
4. Flag all reachable objects with TRANSITIVE_INVALIDATION

Literature confirms: "Many unretracted papers have cited retracted papers" and "it seems critical to re-assess the validity of the claims made in the unretracted papers". "Citation laundering" is a named cause of "Academic Slop" alongside paper mills and compromised peer review.

D.3 Salience Attack Detection

Rule T6: SurfaceAct must log rank scoring, not just inclusion/exclusion.

GraphRAG poisoning attacks demonstrate that "modifying only a few words in the source text can significantly change the constructed graph" and "inject spurious entities and relationships during graph construction". KEPo (Knowledge Evolution Poison) achieves state-of-the-art attack success rates by "forging knowledge evolution events".

Detection: compare rank distributions before/after ingestion. Flag anomalies where a small set of objects dominates the default surface.

D.4 Temporal Cherry-Picking Detection

Rule T7: ScopingAct must record window_justification.

Literature: "The appropriate time window should follow the phenomenon and eligibility criteria rather than an arbitrary rule such as 'the last five years'". PRISMA requires "a rationale and specify the characteristics of evidence sources used for eligibility criteria."

D.5 Acceptance Criterion

\forall j \in \{1, \ldots, 12\}, \;\; \text{AttemptViolation}(I_j) \;\Rightarrow\; \mathrm{Detect} = \top.

Any I_j failing this test is downgraded from "invariant" to "desideratum."

