Appendix E: Validation and Empirical Requirements

E.1 Claim Grammar Pilot (Requirement R1)

Design: Double-annotated extraction of 500 claims from 50 papers in a single domain (boreal fire ecology).

Annotation fields:

· scope (spatial, temporal, population)
· condition (controlled variables)
· exception (boundary conditions)
· modality (assert/hypothesize/report/endorse/contest)

Acceptance threshold:

\kappa_{\text{exception}} \geq 0.6, \quad \kappa_{\text{scope}} \geq 0.6, \quad \kappa_{\text{condition}} \geq 0.6

Literature precedent: claim-extraction inter-annotator studies report \kappa between 0.334 and 0.730 depending on task complexity. The Claim category has an estimated Fleiss' \kappa_C = 0.730 (95% CI, .713 to .746). Values of 0.61–0.80 indicate "substantial agreement".

E.2 PSC Completeness (Requirement R2)

Every PSC value v \in \mathrm{PSC}_t must exhibit:

v \leftarrow \text{projection\_spec} \leftarrow \text{aggregation\_method} \leftarrow \{\text{observations}, \text{datasets}, \text{models}, \text{assessments}\}

If incomplete: \mathrm{flag}(v) = \texttt{PSC\_INCONSISTENCY}.

E.3 Nanopublication Validation

There are "more than 10\mathrm{M} nanopublications publicly accessible worldwide". The neXtProt database published nanopublications for "more than 38K proteins". DisGeNET published as nanopublications with "stable interlinking of the assertion and its metadata by Trusty URIs".

PROV-K extends nanopublications for multi-source assertions, capturing "supporting and conflicting pieces of evidence and truth values". A trust network comprising "over 45,000 facts and four agents" was constructed using LLM agents.

